        "identifier": "https://github.com/mo271/FormalBook/blob/54da1cecd8f3a27faa5d46a23b273611fc4f3365/FormalBook/Chapter_10.lean",
        "note": "FormalBook chapter 10 (pull request #157, merged 6 October 2026; the file at the commit in the id) is a complete, sorry-free Lean proof of Hilbert's third problem by the route of Proofs from THE BOOK (Bricard's condition, Bricard 1896 in sources, the pearl lemma), in a clean abstract model of segments and angles (EdgeData, Decomp). It has the same pair of tetrahedra, T1 and T2, and a theorem headed \"Hilbert's third problem\" for them in that model, and also convex polytopes, faces and a Minkowski-Weyl theorem. Its description says that it does not establish the bridge from arbitrary geometric decompositions to the model, and it has no Dehn invariant. This entry was made independently, by Dehn's invariant, and supplies that bridge for dissections of sets in 3-space into convex polytopes. FormalBook's Minkowski-Weyl theorem is used in one optional file of the development that is not part of this entry. Credit: the theorem was first formalized in Lean there.",
        "relationship": "independent"
      },
